---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
const effective = '8 August 2026';

const permissions = [
    {
        name: 'tabs',
        why: 'Detects tab navigation events.'
    },
    {
        name: 'webNavigation',
        why: 'Observes navigation events so that destination URLs can be checked.'
    },
    {
        name: 'storage',
        why: 'Caches results locally and persists your settings.'
    },
    {
        name: 'https://api.osprey.ac/*',
        why: 'Allows the extension to send URLs to the Osprey proxy server for checking.',
    },
    {
        name: 'https://api.metadefender.com/*',
        why: 'Allows the extension to contact the MetaDefender API directly, only when you configure your own MetaDefender API key.',
    },
];

const localData = [
    {
        name: 'Allowed cache',
        why: 'URLs checked and found safe, stored per provider with an expiration time.',
    },
    {
        name: 'Blocked cache',
        why: 'URLs that were flagged, stored per provider with an expiration time and threat type.',
    },
    {
        name: 'Processing cache',
        why: 'URLs currently being checked to prevent duplicate requests. Stored in-memory only.',
    },
    {
        name: 'Your settings',
        why: 'Enabled providers, cache expiration preferences, and other protection settings.',
    },
];
---

<Base
        title="Privacy Policy - Osprey: Browser Protection"
        description="What data Osprey processes, what it does not, and how it is handled."
>
    <article class="legal">
        <h1>Privacy Policy</h1>
        <p class="meta">Effective date: {effective}</p>

        <hr/>

        <p>
            Osprey: Browser Protection is operated by Osprey Project LLC, a California limited liability company
            ("Osprey Project LLC", "we", "us", or "our"). We are committed to protecting your privacy. This Privacy
            Policy explains what data is processed when you use the Osprey website
            at <a href="https://osprey.ac" class="contact-link">osprey.ac</a>, including the website safety checker
            at <a href="https://osprey.ac/check/" class="contact-link">osprey.ac/check</a>, the Osprey browser
            extension, and its supporting proxy service, and how it is handled.
        </p>

        <p>
            Osprey Project LLC is the data controller responsible for the data described in this policy. Our contact
            details are listed in the Contact section at the end of this page.
        </p>

        <p>The Osprey protection system consists of two main components:</p>

        <ul>
            <li>The <strong>browser extension</strong>, which runs locally on your device</li>
            <li>
                The <strong>proxy server</strong>, hosted in New York, NY at <a href="https://api.osprey.ac"
                                                                                class="contact-link">api.osprey.ac</a>
            </li>
        </ul>

        <h2>How URL checking works</h2>
        <p>
            When you navigate to a website, the browser extension sends the URL to the proxy server, which checks it
            against threat intelligence providers, DNS filtering services, and local threat intelligence lists.
        </p>
        <p>
            <strong>Providers never see which specific websites you visit.</strong> All checks are routed through the
            proxy server, so providers only ever see requests originating from the proxy's IP address, not yours.
            Thousands of users' checks are mixed together from a single IP, making it practically impossible for any
            provider to associate a URL lookup with you specifically.
        </p>
        <p>
            <strong>Most URLs are never sent to providers at all.</strong> The proxy server maintains a cache of
            previously checked URLs. If a URL has already been recently evaluated, the result is returned from cache
            without contacting any provider.
        </p>
        <p>When a URL does reach a provider, the data sent depends on the provider type:</p>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>DNS-based providers</strong> receive only the hostname, never the full URL or path</li>
            <li><strong>API-based providers</strong> receive the full URL with query parameters stripped, aside from the
                limited exceptions described in "Retained query parameters"
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Local threat intelligence lists</strong> are checked entirely in-memory on the proxy server,
                with no external calls per request
            </li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Retained query parameters</h2>
        <p>
            As a narrow, hard-coded exception to the query stripping described above, a small and fixed set of links
            keep specific query parameters. For these links the parameters identify which resource is being checked
            rather than tracking you, and removing them would either make the address meaningless or cause unrelated
            targets to look identical, which would weaken checking. In each case only the named parameters are kept and
            every other parameter is discarded. The exceptions are:
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <code>drive.google.com/uc</code> keeps <code>export</code> and <code>id</code>, which identify the
                Google Drive file being downloaded
            </li>
            <li>
                <code>drive.usercontent.google.com/download</code> keeps <code>id</code> and <code>export</code>, which
                identify the Google Drive file being downloaded
            </li>
            <li>
                <code>google.com/share.google</code> keeps <code>q</code>, which identifies the shared query
            </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
            These exceptions apply consistently wherever a URL is normalized: when the extension checks a link, when the
            website safety checker page checks a link, in the durable scan store, and in false-positive monitoring. The
            retained parameters are the only query data kept for these links, and no other query parameters are
            transmitted, stored, or logged.
        </p>

        <h2>The website and safety checker page</h2>
        <p>
            The Osprey website at <a href="https://osprey.ac" class="contact-link">osprey.ac</a> provides information
            about the project and a website safety checker page
            at <a href="https://osprey.ac/check/" class="contact-link">osprey.ac/check</a>. The website does not require
            an account and does not use advertising or cross-site tracking cookies.
        </p>
        <p>
            The website safety checker page lets you submit a single link to be scanned on demand. When you submit a
            link, it is sent to the proxy server and checked against the same providers, and with the same protections,
            described in "How URL checking works" above. Providers see requests only from the proxy's IP address rather
            than yours, results may be served from the proxy's cache, and query parameters are stripped before a URL
            reaches an API-based provider, aside from the limited exceptions described in "Retained query parameters".
        </p>
        <p>
            A link you submit on the website safety checker page is subject to the same limited operational and
            false-positive monitoring described elsewhere in this policy. It is not used to build a profile of you, and
            it is not sold to any third party.
        </p>
        <p>
            To avoid rescanning the same link repeatedly, the proxy server keeps a durable record of the aggregate
            result for each scanned address. The stored address is normalized first: the scheme is set to https, a
            leading <code>www.</code> is removed, and the query string and fragment are dropped, so tracking parameters
            are not retained, apart from the resource-identifying parameters described in "Retained query parameters".
            The path is kept, so a specific page can be recorded separately from its host. A stored result is served on
            a later scan of the same address for up to 24 hours, after which the link is scanned fresh. This record is
            about the link, not about you: it holds no IP address, no identifier, and nothing that ties the scan to the
            person who ran it.
        </p>
        <p>
            When several vendors independently report a link as phishing or malicious, Osprey may publish a public page
            for that specific link so that others searching for it can see the warning. Only these hard, high-confidence
            detections are published. Links that come back clean, and softer or lower-confidence signals, are never
            published and are excluded from the sitemap and search indexing.
        </p>

        <h2>Bot protection (Cloudflare Turnstile)</h2>
        <p>
            To protect the website safety checker page from automated abuse, we use Cloudflare Turnstile. Turnstile runs
            in invisible mode, so it normally completes without any action from you. To tell humans and bots apart,
            Cloudflare processes certain information from your browser and device when the check page loads and when you
            submit a link.
        </p>
        <p>
            Cloudflare's handling of that information is governed by
            the <a href="https://www.cloudflare.com/en-gb/turnstile-privacy-policy/" class="contact-link">Cloudflare
            Turnstile Privacy Addendum</a>.
        </p>

        <h2>Your IP address</h2>
        <p>
            The proxy server uses your IP address <strong>solely for abuse protections</strong> such as rate limiting.
            For this purpose, your IP is immediately hashed and stored in-memory. Raw IP addresses are not written to
            disk as part of normal proxy operation.
        </p>

        <h2>Third-party integrations</h2>
        <p>
            If you configure a provider using your own API key (for example, MetaDefender), that provider receives
            requests directly from your device rather than through the proxy server. The privacy protections described
            above, meaning IP shielding and caching, <strong>do not apply</strong> to those integrations. You are
            subject to that provider's own data handling practices when using a personal API key.
        </p>

        <h2>Browser permissions</h2>
        <p>The extension requires the following permissions, used strictly for its security features:</p>

        <ul>
            {
                permissions.map((item) => (
                        <li><b>{item.name}</b>: {item.why}</li>
                ))
            }
        </ul>

        <p>
            The extension does <strong>not</strong> request host access to the websites you browse. It cannot read page
            content, inject scripts, or access cookies or form data on the sites you visit. The URLs you navigate to are
            observed only through the browser's navigation events, which expose the destination URL but not the contents
            of the page.
        </p>

        <h2>Data stored locally</h2>
        <p>
            The extension stores the following data in your browser's storage:
        </p>

        <ul>
            {
                localData.map((item) => (
                        <li><b>{item.name}</b>: {item.why}</li>
                ))
            }
        </ul>

        <p>
            All local storage data is cleared when you uninstall the extension, or when you clear the list of excluded
            websites using the settings page. None of it is transmitted to the proxy server or any third party.
        </p>

        <h2>Operational monitoring</h2>
        <p>
            The proxy server uses standard operational tooling (Micrometer, Prometheus, Grafana) to monitor service
            health, stability, and capacity. Metrics include aggregated counts such as requests per provider, cache
            hit/miss rates, and system performance indicators. This data is used solely for service reliability and
            abuse prevention, not for advertising or user profiling.
        </p>

        <h2>False-positive monitoring</h2>
        <p>
            To catch cases where a legitimate website is mistakenly flagged as dangerous, the proxy server keeps a
            transient log entry whenever a provider returns
            a <strong>malicious</strong> or <strong>phishing</strong> verdict. Each entry records only the provider
            name, the verdict, and the same value that was checked against that provider: a hostname for DNS-based
            providers, or the checked URL for URL-based providers. That URL has its query parameters stripped apart from
            the resource-identifying parameters described in "Retained query parameters". It never includes your IP
            address or any other identifier that could tie the lookup back to you.
        </p>
        <p>
            Results that come back safe are <strong>never</strong> logged. These entries exist only in memory, are not
            written to disk, and are not sent to any external system. They are lost when the proxy restarts.
        </p>

        <h2>Data retention</h2>
        <p>
            On the proxy server, rate-limit states, local list snapshots, and transient runtime logs (including the
            false-positive monitoring entries described above) exist only in memory and may be lost on restart.
            Aggregated operational metrics may be retained longer in Prometheus and Grafana, subject to those systems'
            retention settings.
        </p>
        <p>
            The durable scan store described under "The website and safety checker page" is the one component that
            persists across restarts. It holds only normalized link addresses and their aggregate vendor results, with
            no IP addresses or personal identifiers. Records may be updated on a later scan or removed at our
            discretion, and a published warning page is removed when the corresponding link no longer meets the
            high-confidence threshold for publishing.
        </p>

        <h2>Changes to this policy</h2>
        <p>
            We may update this Privacy Policy from time to time. The effective date at the top reflects the most recent
            revision.
        </p>

        <h2>Contact</h2>
        <p>
            For privacy-related questions, contact us at <a href="mailto:support@osprey.ac">support@osprey.ac</a>, or
            write to us at the address below:
        </p>
        <address>
            Osprey Project LLC<br/>
            4225 Executive Square, Suite 600, PMB 6007<br/>
            La Jolla, CA 92037<br/>
            United States
        </address>
    </article>
</Base>
